00:16:46.240 --> 00:16:52.490
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): When we think about security online, we often hear about private and public keys. But what do they actually do

115
00:16:52.570 --> 00:17:01.869
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): at their core. Keys are just long strings of number, but they serve a critical purpose. They allow us to secure communication, prove identity, and establish digital trust.

116
00:17:01.930 --> 00:17:31.100
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Imagine you want to send a secret message to someone. You don't want anyone else to read it. Only the person you intend. This is where public key cryptography comes in the person you're sending the message to gives you their public key. You use that public key to encrypt the message once encrypted, only they can read it because only they have the matching private key. Even if someone intercepts that message along the way, they won't be able to decrypt it. This is how emails, messaging apps, and even online banking keep your data safe.

117
00:17:31.420 --> 00:18:00.199
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): But keys don't just secure messages. They also prove who you are. When you visit a secure website. Your browser checks its digital certificate, which is signed using a private key. If the signature is valid, your browser knows the website is authentic, the same principle applies to digital signatures. When a person or organization signs a document with their private key, anyone can verify that signature using the corresponding public key. This proves the signer's identity without revealing their private key

118
00:18:00.390 --> 00:18:09.490
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): from securing websites and emails to verifying software updates and financial transactions. Public key cryptography is the foundation of digital trust.

119
00:18:09.780 --> 00:18:15.789
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): And as long as your private keys stay private, you remain in control of your own digital identity.

